#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:2
msgid "Loading tasks from a python package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:4
msgid ""
"Usually tasks are declared in a config file such as pyproject.toml, however "
"you can also define tasks in python, and *include* them into your project "
"config via the ``include_script`` global option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:6
msgid ""
"This is primarily intended to make it easier to reuse tasks across projects "
"– by distributing them as a python package. However you can also use it to "
"define tasks with a local script file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:10
msgid "The ``include_script`` global option"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:12
msgid ""
"The following config loads the tasks generated by running the ``get_tasks`` "
"function from the ``mypkg`` package. The package must be accessible to the "
"default :ref:`executor<Configure the executor for a task>`, i.e. it must be "
"within the current project or its dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:14
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"include_script = \"mypkg:get_tasks\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:19
msgid ""
"Just like for the :ref:`include<Loading tasks from another file>` global "
"option, you can also provide a multiple items like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:21
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"include_script = [\"mypkg:get_tasks\", \"something.else:more_tasks\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:28
msgid "Passing arguments to the script"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:30
msgid ""
"The syntax for the function reference is identical to a :doc:`script task<../"
"tasks/task_types/script>`, meaning that it is also possible to pass "
"arguments to the function like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:32
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"include_script = \"mypkg:get_tasks(task_prefix='foo-', "
"exclude_tasks='docs')\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:39
msgid "Specifying a different executor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:41
msgid ""
"Normally Poe the Poet detects the most appropriate :ref:`executor<Configure "
"the executor for a task>` (i.e. project virtualenv) to use to find and "
"execute the referenced package. However you can customize this behavior by "
"specify an executor for the script like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:43
msgid ""
"[[tool.poe.include_script]]\n"
"script   = \"mypkg:get_tasks\"\n"
"executor = { type = \"virtualenv\", location = \"../parent.venv\" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:51
msgid "Setting a working directory for included config"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:53
msgid ""
"You can use the ``cwd`` option on an include_script to make the included "
"tasks run with a different working directory. This option behaves exactly "
"like the :ref:`similar option for include items<Setting a working directory "
"for included tasks>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:55
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"include_script = [\n"
"  { script = \"mypkg:get_tasks\", cwd = \"./subproject1\" }\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:64
msgid "Out-of-the-box tasks from *poethepoet-tasks*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:66
msgid ""
"With ``include_script`` you can reuse common tasks in each new project by "
"depending directly on a private or public *tasks package*. As an example the "
"|poethepoet_tasks_link| package provides an opinionated collection of tasks "
"to get you started, with two simple steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:221
msgid ""
"<a href=\"https://github.com/nat-n/poethepoet-tasks\" "
"target=\"_blank\">poethepoet-tasks</a>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:68
msgid "Add a dev dependency on ``poethepoet-tasks``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:70
msgid "with poetry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:70
msgid "poetry add poethepoet-tasks -G dev"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:75
msgid "with uv"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:75
msgid "uv add --dev poethepoet-tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:80
msgid "Configure your project to include tasks from ``poethepoet-tasks``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:82 ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:161
#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:203
msgid "pyproject.toml"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:82
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"include_script = \"poethepoet_tasks:tasks\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:88
msgid ""
"See the docs for |poethepoet_tasks_link| for details of how you can include "
"just a specific subset of available tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:92
msgid ""
"When you add a dev dependency on a *tasks package* like *poethepoet-tasks* "
"you also automatically get all the other dev dependencies that it requires, "
"e.g. ruff, pytest, etc. *poethepoet-tasks* also comes with config for ruff "
"with opinionated defaults."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:96
msgid "Create your own tasks package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:98
msgid ""
"To create your own tasks package, all you need is a python function that "
"returns the required config structure (as a dict or json string), that can "
"be imported from within your project, either from within the project itself "
"or from a dependency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:102
msgid "Generating config directly"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:104
msgid ""
"The following example illustrates defining a function that can be referenced "
"by ``include_script``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:106
msgid ""
"def generate_tasks():\n"
"  return {\n"
"    \"tasks\": {\n"
"      \"test\": \"pytest\",\n"
"      \"build-proto\": {\n"
"        \"cmd\": \"\"\"\n"
"          protoc --proto_path=schemas --python_out=src/generated schemas/"
"messages.proto\n"
"        \"\"\",\n"
"        \"help\": \"Generate protobuf classes\"\n"
"      },\n"
"      \"build\": {\n"
"        \"sequence\": [\n"
"          \"build-proto\",\n"
"          \"test\",\n"
"          { \"cmd\": \"poetry build\" },\n"
"        ],\n"
"        \"help\": \"Build that code\"\n"
"      }\n"
"    }\n"
"  }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:129
msgid ""
"You may recognise the structure as identical to that supported when :doc:"
"`loading tasks from another file<../guides/include_guide>`, which is a "
"subset of the schema supported in the main project configuration, including "
"``tasks`` and the ``env`` and ``envfile`` :doc:`global options<../"
"global_options>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:131
msgid ""
"There is no restriction on the arguments accepted by the function, or the "
"logic used to generate the tasks. However it is critical that the function "
"does not write anything to stdout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:134
msgid ""
"It is **strongly recommended** to minimize the code that must be imported or "
"executed to generate the tasks configuration. This is because the poe CLI "
"must create a python subprocess to import and execute the function every "
"time it is used within the project; even when generating shell completions. "
"This is also a good reason to ensure there are no side effects of this code "
"or any of its imports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:138
msgid "Defining a TaskCollection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:140
msgid ""
"The recommended way to write tasks in python is to leverage the "
"**TaskCollection** abstraction from |poethepoet_tasks_link|. It provides a "
"convenient API for managing tasks, and allows users to select just the tasks "
"they want via tags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:142
msgid "src/my_tasks/__init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:142
msgid ""
"from poethepoet_tasks import TaskCollection\n"
"\n"
"tasks = TaskCollection()\n"
"\n"
"# Define a simple cmd task\n"
"tasks.add(\n"
"    task_name=\"test\",\n"
"    task_config={\n"
"        \"help\": \"Run project tests\",\n"
"        \"cmd\": \"pytest tests\",\n"
"    },\n"
"    tags=[\"pytest\"], # tags are optional and allow consumers to select "
"only the desired tasks\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:159
msgid ""
"The TaskCollection instance is callable such that it can be referenced "
"directly like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:161
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
"include_script = \"my_tasks:tasks\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:167
msgid "See here for a |example_tasks_link| of creating a TaskCollection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:225
msgid ""
"<a href=\"https://github.com/nat-n/poethepoet-tasks/blob/main/src/"
"poethepoet_tasks/tasks.py\" target=\"_blank\">real world example</a>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:171
msgid "Decorating functions as script tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:173
msgid ""
"A TaskCollection can also be used to define inline :doc:`script tasks<../"
"tasks/task_types/script>`. One nice pattern this enables is to have a `tasks."
"py` in the root of your project, similar to how one would work with *invoke*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:176
msgid "tasks.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:176
msgid ""
"from poethepoet_tasks import TaskCollection\n"
"\n"
"tasks = TaskCollection()\n"
"\n"
"# Define an inline script task\n"
"@tasks.script(tags=[\"example\"])\n"
"def hello(\n"
"    foo: str | None = None,\n"
"    *, # This means preceding args will be positional, instead of CLI "
"options\n"
"    bar: int = 1,\n"
"    baz: bool = True,\n"
"):\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    The first paragraph of the function docstring is picked up as the task "
"help message!\n"
"\n"
"    Args:\n"
"        foo: a positional argument for specifying foo\n"
"        bar: a number of things\n"
"        baz: to baz or not to baz\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:201
msgid ""
"The above example is equivalent to the following toml based configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:203
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.hello]\n"
"script = \"tasks:hello(foo, bar=bar, baz=baz)\"\n"
"help   = \"The first paragraph of the function docstring is picked up as the "
"task help message!\"\n"
"args   = [\n"
"  { name = \"foo\", positional = true, help = \"a positional argument for "
"specifying foo\" },\n"
"  { name = \"bar\", help = \"a number of things\" },\n"
"  { name = \"baz\", help = \"to baz or not to baz\" }\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/packaged_tasks.rst:218
msgid ""
"When creating an inline script task via the TaskCollection decorator most of "
"task options are automatically infered from the code, including task name, "
"args, and help text (given a sufficient docstring)."
msgstr ""
